On Dec 15, 6:56 pm, "3cnf...@gmail.com" <3cnf...@gmail.com> wrote:
> It's always annoyed me that nethack's "energy recharge" potion was so
> rare and so weak. While "confused charging" gives a full mana
> recharge, it might be nice to have an alternative method of recharging
> mana in late game that doesn't involve confusing oneself.

I agree completely.  I take it as an axiom that an item with something
as its stated purpose, like potions of restore ability or gain energy,
should work better than something else that has it only as a side-
effect use (like unicorn horns and scrolls of charging), especially if
the stated-purpose item is a one-time consumable like a potion.

I also agree with Derek's observation that mana recharge should not be
made -too- common.  But potions of gain energy are rare (and have
special mixing applications too), and one-use only, so I would have no
objections to them giving a full power recharge and have charging be
downgraded somewhat (although, as a one-use item, it should still have
a significant effect).

A manasucker enemy wouldn't be bad at all; thinking about it, it's
surprising there's no Nethack monster that drains power, even though
there are those that drain HP, levels, equipment plusses and
intelligence.  I'm not sure that "manasucker" is a good name though.
Is there some monster from early D&D  that could be imported or
retrofitted to match?

By the way, note: newts sometimes grant a small power boost when
eaten.

Another note: the game tendency is to have most drainers respect magic
cancellation, which makes them practically harmless if the player's
wearing a simple elven cloak.  I'd think to make the monster (and
other drainers already in the game) strong enough to matter, MC will
have to be made less powerful.

- John H.

